# AutoPilot BI - Agent Constitution
# This file governs how AI coding agents and the application's own agents behave.

## Project Context
- AutoPilot BI: AI Business Operating System — Multi-Module Business Process Automation
- Track A: Business Process Automation (Deploy or Die hackathon)
- Stack: FastAPI backend, SQLite/PostgreSQL, Pandas, scikit-learn, Gemini (narrative only)
- Active modules: Inventory & Sales Intelligence, HR Automation
- Planned modules: Finance Automation, Operations Intelligence

## Architectural Rules (Non-Negotiable)

### Rule 1: Audit Everything
Every agent MUST inherit from BaseAgent and call log_action() at every step.
No agent may perform an action without producing an audit_log entry.
If there is no audit entry, the action did not happen.

### Rule 2: Human Approval Required
ALL recommendations MUST be created with status="pending".
No recommendation transitions to "approved" without an explicit human API call.
ReportAgent MUST refuse to run if zero recommendations are approved.
This rule applies identically to ALL modules — inventory and HR.

### Rule 3: Deterministic Computation
All numerical outputs (revenue, growth %, dead stock count, Business Health Score, fit scores)
MUST come from Pandas or SQL operations. Never from LLM inference.
LLM (Gemini) is permitted ONLY for converting computed facts into plain-language narrative.

### Rule 4: Traceable Metadata
Every API response that contains analysis data MUST include dataset_id and agent_name
so the consumer can trace the data back to its source.

### Rule 5: No Silent Failures
All error states MUST return clear, user-readable error messages.
No bare except blocks. No swallowed exceptions. No empty error responses.
If an agent cannot complete its work, it logs the failure and raises an exception.

### Rule 6: Module Independence
Each module (inventory, hr, finance) MUST use the shared infrastructure:
- Same BaseAgent for audit logging
- Same Recommendation model for action items
- Same AuditLog model for traceability
- Same approval endpoints for HITL
New modules MUST NOT require changes to existing modules or shared infrastructure.

## Code Style
- Python 3.11+
- Type hints required on all function signatures
- Docstrings on all classes and public methods
- Use logging module, not print statements
- SQLAlchemy 2.0 style queries
- Ruff for linting (line length 120)

## Database Rules
- SQLite for local development, PostgreSQL for production
- All tables have created_at timestamps
- Foreign keys enforced
- Audit log is APPEND-ONLY: never update or delete records
- Use get_db() dependency injection for all database access
- The `module` field on Dataset, Recommendation, and AuditLog identifies the owning module

## Agent Development Rules
- Each agent is a class inheriting from BaseAgent
- Each agent implements run() as its entry point
- Agents call reusable skill functions from analysis/ or modules/ packages
- Agents do not call other agents directly — the API layer orchestrates the pipeline
- New business rules go in InsightAgent (inventory) or module-specific agents (HR), not in analysis modules
  (analysis modules compute facts, agents interpret them)

## Module Development Rules
- Module agents live in app/modules/{module_name}/ directory
- Module API routes live in app/api/{module_name}.py
- Module models are added to app/models.py (single source of truth for schema)
- Module sample data generators live in samples/generate_{module}_data.py
- Module tests live in tests/test_{module}.py
- Every new module MUST have at least one end-to-end pipeline test

## Testing Rules
- pytest for all tests
- Every agent must have at least one test
- Tests use in-memory SQLite, not the dev database
- Sample data fixtures in conftest.py
- CI pipeline must genuinely pass (no || true)

## Git Workflow
- Commit messages reference story IDs: feat(US-XX): description
- Prefixes: feat:|fix:|docs:|test:|refactor:|ci:
- Never commit .env files, API keys, or database files
- Progressive commits — not a single dump

